From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 02:33:45 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] newlib: minimal port for pdp11-uknc-rt11

Adds a small newlib port targeting RT-11 on real UKNC hardware
(pdp11-uknc-rt11, see gcc/config.gcc in the wdigger/gcc fork), so
ordinary printf/malloc/file-I/O-style C code works out of the box
instead of every example hand-rolling its own tiny libc.

- libc/sys/rt11/syscalls.c: _read/_write/_open/_close/_lseek/_fstat/
  _isatty/_sbrk/_exit, plus ENOSYS stubs for _kill/_getpid/_link/
  _unlink/_stat. Console I/O for fds 0/1/2 goes through RT-11's own
  TTY EMTs (.TTYIN/.TTYOUT), with the value pinned to hardware
  register R0 via GCC's register-asm extension (a plain "r" constraint
  isn't enough here -- the EMT reads/writes R0 specifically).  _sbrk
  grows the heap from _end (as defined by the linker script) upward,
  capped against the current SP with a fixed safety margin. Startup
  itself is NOT provided here -- it comes from GCC's own
  STARTFILE_SPEC (crt0rt.o/parse_args.o), so configure.host sets
  have_crt0="no" for this arm.
- configure.host: sys_dir=rt11 for pdp11-uknc-rt11; libc/acinclude.m4
  and libc/sys/Makefile.inc wire the new sys/rt11 directory into the
  generated build the same way every other sys_dir does.  A separate
  pdp11* arm in the CPU-dispatch case avoids falling through to the
  generic default, which sets -DMISSING_SYSCALL_NAMES and would alias
  away this port's _read/_write/etc. (see _syslist.h) in favor of
  plain names it never defines -- this port uses the normal
  underscore-prefixed convention (matching e.g. d10v), not the
  plain-name one.
- libc/include/machine/ieeefp.h: a __pdp11__ block defining
  __IEEE_LITTLE_ENDIAN (doubles are little-endian at the word level on
  this target even though plain 32-bit ints are word-swapped) and
  __SMALL_BITFIELDS (pdp11 int is 16 bits, same reason msp430/rl78/cr16
  need it) -- without these this header hits a flat #error and, once
  that's fixed, bitfield-width errors on 32-bit-wide fields.
- libc/ssp/Makefile.inc: stack_protector.c left out. It needs
  __attribute__((constructor)) to seed __stack_chk_guard at startup,
  and GCC's pdp11 backend doesn't support constructors at all ("sorry,
  unimplemented: global constructors not supported on this target") --
  a real, separate backend limitation, not something newlib can work
  around. -fstack-protector isn't usable on this target either way, so
  nothing else references __stack_chk_guard/__stack_chk_fail without
  it.

Verified with a full from-scratch build of gcc+newlib (--with-newlib):
libc.a/libm.a build and install cleanly with zero compiler ICEs and
zero archive errors, and a real `printf`/`malloc`/`hypot` test program
compiles and links straight to a working RT-11 .sav file with a single
plain `pdp11-uknc-rt11-gcc -O2 -o test.sav test.c` -- no -nodefaultlibs,
no manual -lgcc.  (Two genuine, separate GCC pdp11-backend bugs were
found and fixed along the way in the wdigger/gcc fork rather than
worked around here: a reload-pass ICE avoided by -msoft-float, now the
default for this vendor, and a cprop_hardreg ICE in
libc/stdlib/gdtoa-hexnan.c fixed at its root with a new predicate in
gcc/config/pdp11/predicates.md.)

Includes what was a separate fix on top of this one, now part of it:

rt11: remove redundant errno definition, matching reent.c's own default

libc/sys/rt11/syscalls.c copied the "#undef errno / int errno;" pattern
from its own stated reference port, libc/sys/d10v/syscalls.c. But
libc/reent/reent.c already provides that exact definition by default,
guarded by #ifndef REENTRANT_SYSCALLS_PROVIDED -- precisely the escape
hatch a port is meant to use if (and only if) it wants to own errno's
storage itself. rt11's port never defines that macro, so both
translation units emitted a real, non-tentative `errno` symbol,
producing a "multiple definition" link error.

This only surfaces under a modern (GCC >= 10) toolchain: -fno-common
has been the default since then, so tentative definitions across files
are no longer silently merged the way older GCC always did -- which is
why d10v itself, never rebuilt with a current GCC, never hit this.

Removing the duplicate leaves errno as newlib's normal (*__errno())
macro, backed by reent.c's default storage, same as every other
assignment already in this file (errno = ENOSYS; etc.).
---
 newlib/configure.host                |  46 ++++++
 newlib/libc/acinclude.m4             |   2 +-
 newlib/libc/include/machine/ieeefp.h |  11 ++
 newlib/libc/ssp/Makefile.inc         |  11 +-
 newlib/libc/sys/Makefile.inc         |   3 +
 newlib/libc/sys/rt11/Makefile.inc    |   1 +
 newlib/libc/sys/rt11/syscalls.c      | 211 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 7 files changed, 282 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 newlib/libc/sys/rt11/Makefile.inc
 create mode 100644 newlib/libc/sys/rt11/syscalls.c

diff --git a/newlib/configure.host b/newlib/configure.host
index 960cca2..b1799b5 100644
--- a/newlib/configure.host
+++ b/newlib/configure.host
@@ -293,6 +293,33 @@ case "${host_cpu}" in
 	machine_dir=or1k
 	have_init_fini=no
 	;;
+  pdp11*)
+	# No CPU-specific optimized routines in libc/machine; plain C
+	# implementations are used throughout.
+	#
+	# No -msoft-float here: the absence of an FPU is a trait of the
+	# uknc vendor, not of this CPU generically or of the rt11 OS this
+	# port targets, so it belongs in the compiler's own target defaults
+	# (gcc/common/config/pdp11/pdp11-common.cc, gated on
+	# TARGET_UKNC_DEFAULTS) rather than hardcoded for every pdp11 build
+	# here. Real UKNC hardware has no FPU coprocessor, and GCC now
+	# defaults to -msoft-float for that vendor accordingly.
+	#
+	# That default is still functionally required, though, to avoid a
+	# real backend bug: a union of a double with a smaller integer
+	# struct member (exactly what libm/math/e_hypot.c,
+	# libm/common/s_cbrt.c and libm/common/s_infinity.c all do via the
+	# GET_HIGH_WORD-style type-punning macros) makes GCC represent the
+	# union as a single DImode pseudo that also needs a DFmode subreg
+	# view for hardware floating-point registers, and reload can't find
+	# a valid allocation for that combination on pdp11's tiny register
+	# file -- "internal compiler error: in find_reloads_toplev, at
+	# reload.cc:4760".  -msoft-float avoids it by keeping doubles out of
+	# the separate FP register class entirely.  If this newlib port is
+	# ever built for a non-uknc pdp11 target (hardware FPU enabled by
+	# default), this ICE will resurface and need -msoft-float added back
+	# here explicitly.
+	;;
   powerpc*)
 	machine_dir=powerpc
 	libm_machine_dir=powerpc
@@ -447,6 +474,13 @@ case "${host}" in
 	sys_dir=tirtos
 	have_crt0="no"
 	;;
+  pdp11-*-rt11*)
+	sys_dir=rt11
+	# crt0 (crt0rt.o) is supplied by GCC's own STARTFILE_SPEC
+	# (gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h, TARGET_RT11_DEFAULTS), built as part
+	# of libgcc (libgcc/config/pdp11/t-pdp11rt11) -- not by newlib.
+	have_crt0="no"
+	;;
   *-esp-*)
 	sys_dir=esp
     have_crt0="no"
@@ -815,6 +849,18 @@ newlib_cflags="${newlib_cflags} -DCLOCK_PROVIDED -DMALLOC_PROVIDED -DEXIT_PROVID
   or1k*|or1knd*)
 	syscall_dir=syscalls
 	;;
+  pdp11*)
+	# Without an explicit arm here, this would fall through to the
+	# generic default below and pick up -DMISSING_SYSCALL_NAMES, which
+	# makes libc/reent's _read_r-style wrappers call the plain (e.g.
+	# "read") syscall name instead of the underscore-prefixed one
+	# (_syslist.h's #define _read read etc.) -- but libc/sys/rt11/
+	# syscalls.c uses the normal underscore-prefixed convention (_read,
+	# _write, ...), the same as e.g. d10v above, so nothing here should
+	# be aliased.  syscall_dir is left unset (no libc/syscalls): that
+	# directory only adds plain-POSIX-name wrappers (read() calling
+	# _read_r()) on top, which nothing in this minimal port needs.
+	;;
   powerpc*-*-eabialtivec*)
 	default_newlib_io_long_long="yes"
 	newlib_cflags="${newlib_cflags} -DMISSING_SYSCALL_NAMES"
diff --git a/newlib/libc/acinclude.m4 b/newlib/libc/acinclude.m4
index 52a6c57..0a2f7dc 100644
--- a/newlib/libc/acinclude.m4
+++ b/newlib/libc/acinclude.m4
@@ -19,7 +19,7 @@ m4_foreach_w([SYS_DIR], [
   m88kbug mmixware
   netware
   or1k
-  rdos rtems
+  rdos rt11 rtems
   sh sysmec sysnec810 sysnecv850 sysvi386 sysvnecv70
   tic80 tirtos
   w65
diff --git a/newlib/libc/include/machine/ieeefp.h b/newlib/libc/include/machine/ieeefp.h
index c5fafcf..1412a57 100644
--- a/newlib/libc/include/machine/ieeefp.h
+++ b/newlib/libc/include/machine/ieeefp.h
@@ -212,6 +212,17 @@
 # define _SUPPORTS_ERREXCEPT
 #endif
 
+/* PDP-11 doubles are little-endian at the word level (GCC's own
+   __FLOAT_WORD_ORDER__ == __ORDER_LITTLE_ENDIAN__ for this target),
+   even though plain 32-bit integers are word-swapped ("PDP-endian",
+   __BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_PDP_ENDIAN__) -- the two are independent
+   here and only the float one matters to this file.  __SMALL_BITFIELDS:
+   pdp11 int is 16 bits, same reason msp430/rl78/cr16 above need it.  */
+#ifdef __pdp11__
+#define __IEEE_LITTLE_ENDIAN
+#define __SMALL_BITFIELDS	/* 16 Bit INT */
+#endif
+
 #ifdef __riscv
 #if defined(__BYTE_ORDER__) && (__BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_BIG_ENDIAN__)
 #define __IEEE_BIG_ENDIAN
diff --git a/newlib/libc/ssp/Makefile.inc b/newlib/libc/ssp/Makefile.inc
index f1dc912..533fa8d 100644
--- a/newlib/libc/ssp/Makefile.inc
+++ b/newlib/libc/ssp/Makefile.inc
@@ -1,7 +1,14 @@
 ## General sources.
+# stack_protector.c is deliberately left out for pdp11: it needs a global
+# constructor (__attribute__((constructor))) to seed __stack_chk_guard at
+# startup, and GCC's pdp11 backend doesn't support constructors at all
+# ("sorry, unimplemented: global constructors not supported on this
+# target") -- a real, separate backend limitation, not something newlib
+# can work around.  -fstack-protector isn't usable on this target either
+# way, so nothing references __stack_chk_guard/__stack_chk_fail without
+# it.
 %C%_ELIX_SOURCES = \
-  %D%/chk_fail.c \
-  %D%/stack_protector.c
+  %D%/chk_fail.c
 
 ## String sources.
 %C%_ELIX_SOURCES += \
diff --git a/newlib/libc/sys/Makefile.inc b/newlib/libc/sys/Makefile.inc
index 9f87589..055769e 100644
--- a/newlib/libc/sys/Makefile.inc
+++ b/newlib/libc/sys/Makefile.inc
@@ -34,6 +34,9 @@ endif
 if HAVE_LIBC_SYS_RDOS_DIR
 include %D%/rdos/Makefile.inc
 endif
+if HAVE_LIBC_SYS_RT11_DIR
+include %D%/rt11/Makefile.inc
+endif
 if HAVE_LIBC_SYS_RTEMS_DIR
 include %D%/rtems/Makefile.inc
 endif
diff --git a/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/Makefile.inc b/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/Makefile.inc
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ec43972
--- /dev/null
+++ b/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/Makefile.inc
@@ -0,0 +1 @@
+libc_a_SOURCES += %D%/syscalls.c
diff --git a/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/syscalls.c b/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/syscalls.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..fe990e4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/newlib/libc/sys/rt11/syscalls.c
@@ -0,0 +1,211 @@
+/* syscalls.c -- minimal newlib syscall layer for RT-11 (pdp11-uknc-rt11)
+ *
+ * This is a small port in newlib's own sense (see libc/sys/d10v for the
+ * precedent this follows): one syscalls.c, no reentrancy support, no
+ * POSIX path translation.  Startup (crt0) is not provided here -- it
+ * comes from GCC's own STARTFILE_SPEC (gcc/config/pdp11/pdp11.h,
+ * TARGET_RT11_DEFAULTS), built via libgcc/config/pdp11/t-pdp11rt11 from
+ * crt0rt.s/parse_args.c -- see newlib/configure.host's have_crt0=no for
+ * this OS.
+ *
+ * Console I/O (fds 0/1/2) goes through RT-11's own terminal EMTs
+ * (.TTYIN/EMT 340, .TTYOUT/EMT 341 -- see the RT-11 System Macro Library
+ * Manual's "Programmed Request Description and Examples" chapter for
+ * both).  Real file I/O (.LOOKUP/.ENTER/.READW/.WRITW/.CLOSE) is not
+ * implemented yet: RT-11's file requests are block/word-oriented, not
+ * byte-stream oriented like POSIX read()/write(), so mapping one onto
+ * the other needs its own buffering layer rather than a one-line EMT
+ * wrapper -- left as a followup once this console+heap baseline is
+ * verified working.  _open/_close/_lseek on anything other than the
+ * three console fds fail with ENOSYS in the meantime.
+ */
+
+#include <_ansi.h>
+#include <sys/types.h>
+#include <sys/stat.h>
+#include <errno.h>
+
+/* .EXIT -- EMT 350 (same request crt0rt.s itself uses to return to
+   RT-11 after main() returns).  */
+void
+_exit (int status)
+{
+  asm volatile ("emt 0350");
+  for (;;)
+    ;
+}
+
+/* .TTYOUT -- EMT 341.  Character in R0; BCS retries while the console
+   is still busy transmitting the previous one.  Both EMTs read/write R0
+   specifically, not just "some register", so it's pinned by name (same
+   trick this file's own _sbrk uses for the stack pointer) rather than
+   left to a generic "r" constraint, which wouldn't guarantee R0.  */
+static void
+rt11_ttyout (char c)
+{
+  register unsigned short r0 asm ("r0") = (unsigned char) c;
+  asm volatile (
+    "1: emt 0341\n\t"
+    "bcs 1b"
+    : "+r" (r0)
+    :
+    : "cc");
+}
+
+/* .TTYIN -- EMT 340.  Blocks until a character is available; returned
+   right-justified (low byte) in R0.  */
+static char
+rt11_ttyin (void)
+{
+  register unsigned short r0 asm ("r0");
+  asm volatile (
+    "1: emt 0340\n\t"
+    "bcs 1b"
+    : "=r" (r0));
+  return (char) r0;
+}
+
+int
+_write (int file, const void *ptr, size_t len)
+{
+  const char *p = (const char *) ptr;
+  size_t i;
+
+  if (file != 1 && file != 2)
+    {
+      errno = ENOSYS;
+      return -1;
+    }
+
+  for (i = 0; i < len; i++)
+    rt11_ttyout (p[i]);
+
+  return (int) len;
+}
+
+int
+_read (int file, void *ptr, size_t len)
+{
+  char *p = (char *) ptr;
+  size_t i;
+
+  if (file != 0)
+    {
+      errno = ENOSYS;
+      return -1;
+    }
+
+  for (i = 0; i < len; i++)
+    {
+      char c = rt11_ttyin ();
+
+      p[i] = c;
+      if (c == '\r' || c == '\n')
+	{
+	  i++;
+	  break;
+	}
+    }
+
+  return (int) i;
+}
+
+int
+_close (int file)
+{
+  if (file == 0 || file == 1 || file == 2)
+    return 0;
+  errno = ENOSYS;
+  return -1;
+}
+
+int
+_lseek (int file, off_t offset, int whence)
+{
+  errno = ENOSYS;
+  return -1;
+}
+
+int
+_open (const char *name, int flags, int mode)
+{
+  errno = ENOSYS;
+  return -1;
+}
+
+int
+_fstat (int file, struct stat *st)
+{
+  st->st_mode = S_IFCHR;
+  return 0;
+}
+
+int
+_isatty (int file)
+{
+  return file == 0 || file == 1 || file == 2;
+}
+
+/* Heap grows from _end (see ld/scripttempl/pdp11rt11.sc) up towards the
+   current stack, with a fixed safety margin -- the same class of
+   stack/heap collision this project already hit once with a hardcoded
+   crt0 stack address, not repeating it here.  */
+#define RT11_STACK_MARGIN 512
+
+register char *stack_ptr asm ("sp");
+
+caddr_t
+_sbrk (int incr)
+{
+  extern char _end;
+  static char *heap_end;
+  char *prev_heap_end;
+
+  if (heap_end == 0)
+    heap_end = &_end;
+
+  prev_heap_end = heap_end;
+
+  if (heap_end + incr > stack_ptr - RT11_STACK_MARGIN)
+    {
+      errno = ENOMEM;
+      return (caddr_t) -1;
+    }
+
+  heap_end += incr;
+  return (caddr_t) prev_heap_end;
+}
+
+int
+_kill (int pid, int sig)
+{
+  errno = ENOSYS;
+  return -1;
+}
+
+int
+_getpid (void)
+{
+  return 1;
+}
+
+int
+_link (const char *old, const char *new)
+{
+  errno = ENOSYS;
+  return -1;
+}
+
+int
+_unlink (const char *name)
+{
+  errno = ENOSYS;
+  return -1;
+}
+
+int
+_stat (const char *file, struct stat *st)
+{
+  errno = ENOSYS;
+  return -1;
+}
-- 
